so far the only confirmed TV leaders  debate of this election is a  head-to-head between Jeremy Corbyn and  Boris Johnson the German leader Joe  Swenson says that's unacceptable he's  prepared to take legal action against  ITV to challenge the decision the TV  debate about who will be the next prime  minister of our country cannot and  should not exclude the only woman leader  who is able to be the next prime  minister because I stand as a candidate  to be Prime Minister and as the leader  of the biggest and strongest party of  remain it is a nonsense to suggest that  these debates should go ahead leave  versus leave with no voice for remain  whatsoever television debates with all  the main national party leaders have  only happened once that was in 2010 Sky  News is now hoping to replicate that  with a three-way leaders debate at the  end of November in a statement the head  of Sky News John Riley said Sky News  originally proposed a debate between the  leaders of the current government and  the opposition however as there are now  multiple proposals for double headed  debates we believe it is sensible to  suggest an alternative approach that  will allow the public to properly  scrutinize a wider range of views we  will continue to provide appropriate  coverage to other parties in our  broadcasts over the election period the  Lib Dem leader accepted the invitation  immediately but no response has yet been  received from the other two leaders and  concern has been raised by both the SNP  in the brexit party that they too should  be invited  last year Sky News called for the  establishment of an independent debate  commission to be backed by parliament  and funded by broadcasters the Liberal  Democrats say the current argument over  debates shows why such a commission is  necessary every year there is a  kerfuffle about are they going to happen  how are they going to happen what are  the rules by which you know the idea  that we are now in a position where  we're looking to take ITV to court  possibly over  it just shouldn't happen so if we had an  independent commission that interpreted  the rules set out by Ofcom independently  in advance then I think it would just be  so much calmer previously the idea of an  independent commission won the support  of both mr corbyn and mr. johnson we've  always wanted debates between leaders  not just the party leaders but also with  the spokespersons for different  departments and so that there is an open  public debate I've certainly support  Skye's campaign to have an independent  american-style Commission - to make sure  we get it right but since the December  election was confirmed labour and the  Conservatives have not shown any  inclination to take part in debates  unless they are head-to-head without  other party leaders representative Tom  Rayner Sky News Westminster
 

